**Dual-write (started 2026-11-09).** The service still writes every create, update and cancel to legacy Postgres and reads only from legacy. Each write also reaches orders-v2 asynchronously. The service commits an outbox row in the same legacy transaction, and `outbox-relay` applies those rows in order. `backfill_orders_v2` copies historical orders overnight and takes about 11 nights. `order-diff` compares the two stores hourly. This phase ends when:
- the backfill is complete;
- mismatches stay under 0.01% and relay lag p99 stays under 2 seconds, both for 3 days;
- a one-time full-table checksum matches.

**Cutover.** Reads move first. The `orders.read_from_v2` flag goes to 1%, 10%, 50% and 100%, one step a day, while writes still go to both stores. Reads then soak at 100% for 48 hours. Next, `orders.write_legacy` is turned off, so orders-v2 is the only store receiving writes, and `outbox-relay` is scaled to zero. Legacy stays read-only for 30 days and is then decommissioned. The cutover counts as done after 7 days with no `ORD-4xx` incident and order-history p99 at or under 180 ms.

**Rollback.** Until the write stop, rollback is setting `orders.read_from_v2` back to 0, because legacy has received every write. After the write stop there is no sync back to legacy, so falling back would lose every order written since. That is why the write stop needs sign-off from the DBA lead and the commerce director.
